#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068508 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "f374b567-9a69-57ae-88eb-644ebfc451d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-506" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int  \\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\nx^2+3x-4 = (x-1)(x+4) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} & =\\frac{5x}{(x-1)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =A(x+4)+B(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4) & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-1) \\\\ -20 & =-5B \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, by plugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1) & =A(1+4)+B(1-1) \\\\ 5 & =5A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4}  = \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)} +\\dfrac{4}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{4}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "fce14198-c8ee-50a6-981d-85df79cce761" :question/math-academy-id "e-507" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2x+3}{(2x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =A(2x-1)+B(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 4 & =2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(2x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9c952b5-b0b8-5452-8c02-cc50c1112f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329991} {:answer/id #uuid "9313535f-7431-5830-bd3d-58895013ec28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9313535f-7431-5830-bd3d-58895013ec28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329992} :answer-field/id #uuid "19e6f951-0205-55ba-824c-bc46c458c916" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329990} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b34bcff9-df4c-5706-87b3-081d015c2a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34bcff9-df4c-5706-87b3-081d015c2a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329994} :answer-field/id #uuid "08ee7f52-3d9e-53df-8993-e91d3408c9c2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3ce1874-816d-5616-9c10-d203d3f4b0a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-348898" :question/problem "Evaluate the following indefinite integral and fill in the missing coefficients.\n\n$\\int \\frac{7x-11}{(x-8)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-8|\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-8)(x+1).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7x-11}{(x-8)(x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x-8}+\\frac{B}{x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n7x-11=A(x+1)+B(x-8).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=8,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(8)-11 & =A(8+1)+B(8-8) \\\\ 45 & =9A \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-1)-11 & =A(-1+1)+B(-1-8) \\\\ -18 & =-9B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7x-11}{(x-8)(x+1)}=\\frac{5}{x-8}+\\frac{2}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7x-11}{(x-8)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{1}{x-8}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-8|+2\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7cb62f1-624b-5572-8b9d-2e57dd7cb8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7cb62f1-624b-5572-8b9d-2e57dd7cb8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329997} :answer-field/id #uuid "102cb884-e241-59e7-8d95-bc521e1d32c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329996} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07099bbf-40db-554d-af1a-de896fb2e589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07099bbf-40db-554d-af1a-de896fb2e589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329999} :answer-field/id #uuid "522f3f76-4d45-55c6-9c0d-f0605d17a519" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16dae2d4-d8c2-58dc-951e-8924c446f8fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-348933" :question/problem "Evaluate the following indefinite integral and fill in the missing coefficients.\n\n$\\int \\frac{11}{(2x+5)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+5|\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+C$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x+5)(x-2).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{(2x+5)(x-2)} & =\\frac{A}{2x+5}+\\frac{B}{x-2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n11=A(x-2)+B(2x+5).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =9B \\\\ B & =\\frac{11}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{5}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =-\\frac{9}{2}A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{22}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{(2x+5)(x-2)}=-\\frac{22}{9(2x+5)}+\\frac{11}{9(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11}{(2x+5)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{22}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{11}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{9}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+5|+\\frac{11}{9}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "483f7011-c7aa-512d-87b8-a19f9958a524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330002} {:answer/id #uuid "460331c6-d06c-57b0-8fd2-3ca938fc55fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "483f7011-c7aa-512d-87b8-a19f9958a524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330002} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f01bd9-0772-5dd9-979c-19468dbb5bd4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90a8232e-fe3a-5d08-9a00-221f3761736e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348976" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x-5=(x+5)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5} & =\\frac{5x-1}{(x+5)(x-1)}=\\frac{A}{x+5}+\\frac{B}{x-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x-1=A(x-1)+B(x+5).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)-1 & =A(1-1)+B(1+5) \\\\ 4 & =6B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-5,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-5)-1 & =A(-5-1)+B(-5+5) \\\\ -26 & =-6A \\\\ A & =\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}=\\frac{13}{3(x+5)}+\\frac{2}{3(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{13}{3(x+5)}+\\frac{2}{3(x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|]}_{-4}^{0} \\\\  & =(\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))-(\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cfb5d8c-8454-5d20-9a85-a0ce83e2d18a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{49}{48})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cfb5d8c-8454-5d20-9a85-a0ce83e2d18a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{49}{48})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330006} :answer-field/id #uuid "6228426c-c1df-5f2b-b917-c412d67493dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "047f5aca-d84b-5888-b35a-81b77ae7adbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-349040" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n12{x}^{2}-x-6=(4x-3)(3x+2).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6} & =\\frac{36x-10}{(4x-3)(3x+2)}=\\frac{A}{4x-3}+\\frac{B}{3x+2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n36x-10=A(3x+2)+B(4x-3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{3}{4},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(\\frac{3}{4})-10 & =A(3(\\frac{3}{4})+2)+B(4(\\frac{3}{4})-3) \\\\ 17 & =\\frac{17}{4}A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(-\\frac{2}{3})-10 & =A(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2)+B(4(-\\frac{2}{3})-3) \\\\ -34 & =-\\frac{17}{3}B \\\\ B & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}=\\frac{4}{4x-3}+\\frac{6}{3x+2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{4x-3}+\\frac{6}{3x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-3|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-3|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(4x-3)(3x+2)^{2}|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(4x-3)(3x+2)^{2}|{|}_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{48}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{48}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "725be603-15ff-5875-b99e-abd4b9f3e038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316416} {:answer/id #uuid "6d687db5-537f-5253-8bd2-1cb184f94320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{121}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316417} {:answer/id #uuid "e131523c-815a-54eb-8aac-8845ae79f21d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{2}{121})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d687db5-537f-5253-8bd2-1cb184f94320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{121}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316417} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b2b73e0-5c91-54e3-976b-a42f3d542b56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14cfb232-8881-5dc6-aba7-20fb95844fe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-349041" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-19x-4=(5x+1)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4} & =\\frac{15x-39}{(5x+1)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{5x+1}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n15x-39=A(x-4)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(4)-39 & =A(4-4)+B(5(4)+1) \\\\ 21 & =21B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(-\\frac{1}{5})-39 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}-4)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ -42 & =-\\frac{21}{5}A \\\\ A & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}=\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(242)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{242}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{121}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ab0f3a-4e41-524f-bd9e-03c46311722b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067332} {:answer/id #uuid "c255a472-9b1a-56de-baec-93fc501ee751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067333} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f7b7a9-f5dc-5e8b-81ea-22c5213943d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067334} {:answer/id #uuid "17efe871-acac-5c1b-affc-45c1fa349a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067335} {:answer/id #uuid "4b41ab18-e879-5684-9158-ed41d186f5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c255a472-9b1a-56de-baec-93fc501ee751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067333} :answer-field/id #uuid "c334a28a-25dc-5066-8861-a6d8339b2304" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f6ebcba-4e39-5954-8ee8-c37c592d0f6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49604" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C,\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)} & =\\frac{A}{3x+2}+\\frac{B}{2x-3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n13=A(2x-3)+B(3x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(\\frac{3}{2})-3)+B(3(\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\ 13 & =\\frac{13}{2}B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(-\\frac{2}{3})-3)+B(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2) \\\\ 13 & =-\\frac{13}{3}A \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}=-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{3}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,b=-1,$ and $a+b=1-1=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3affa57-2258-54f8-b3f1-f7b03d929bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330010} {:answer/id #uuid "0c15b682-54ba-583f-b3a3-0e98a586679c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330011} {:answer/id #uuid "2664f4aa-a1dc-51f5-ad32-c5c8d0577db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330012} {:answer/id #uuid "475f4de0-d7ee-5a39-8286-6799cb3114bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330013} {:answer/id #uuid "93a85711-e1c9-5c86-8690-c53ec526beac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2664f4aa-a1dc-51f5-ad32-c5c8d0577db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330012} :answer-field/id #uuid "2469b527-adf4-519b-9594-48dd0849db3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c679d3d1-b950-5f64-a26b-57b3e4e6ca7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-49764" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}=\\frac{A}{(x+7)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x+29=A(x+4)+B(x+7).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4)+29 & =A(-4+4)+B(-4+7) \\\\ 9 & =3B \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-7,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-7)+29 & =A(-7+4)+B(-7+7) \\\\ -6 & =-3A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}=\\frac{2}{(x+7)}+\\frac{3}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x+7}+\\frac{3}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x+7}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+7|+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)^{3}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)^{3}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|{|}_{-6}^{-5} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "771dfcc1-f6c3-59ed-8961-3fe477b011b6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "562475c1-e434-5753-8042-2c41d767c445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330017} {:answer/id #uuid "dc693f8f-4f80-5de6-b23a-c6e99a4d8224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330018} {:answer/id #uuid "11efaf85-1aef-5f48-b369-35549de3bc38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330019} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5fd2cc-2907-5150-b596-3ae683c6b16b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330020} {:answer/id #uuid "bb83e933-4eb8-5113-b1d3-b32068ecb7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc693f8f-4f80-5de6-b23a-c6e99a4d8224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330018} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c228321-9111-5d70-9524-860262e81cab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9186ef7b-f943-5612-ab7d-546d5deb9817" :question/math-academy-id "q-49769" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx+1=A(x+3)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+1 & =A(-3+3)+B(-3) \\\\ -2 & =-3B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+1 & =A(0+3)+B(0) \\\\ 1 & =3A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}=\\frac{1}{3x}+\\frac{2}{3(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3x}+\\frac{2}{3(x+3)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x(x+3)^{2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x(x+3)^{2}|{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "823fef91-4b8a-517d-85f7-21e44fa5231b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330024} {:answer/id #uuid "4ddcd1b4-11fa-575f-bd1d-9ee3213f0953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330025} {:answer/id #uuid "94aff6cc-ad04-5afb-ba0a-51dbb7925846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330026} {:answer/id #uuid "42d05105-e872-579a-8c3b-8025f05506eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330027} {:answer/id #uuid "cad5fd07-0ca0-5bc8-a23e-dca48f4c0370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ddcd1b4-11fa-575f-bd1d-9ee3213f0953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330025} :answer-field/id #uuid "4239dd8c-1ffc-52b1-9e7b-8119073af6c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "220a8b03-1c32-5c37-9106-3fe4303bad77" :question/math-academy-id "q-49905" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C,\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x-1=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-\\frac{1}{2})-1 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3)-1 & =A(2(3)+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 14 & =7A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{2}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{2x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54bab77-d77f-5275-b454-ddb565eaa280" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indefinite Integral of a Rational Function Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4635e99-37ab-53ed-a275-dadd0a021879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330031} {:answer/id #uuid "db292bc9-5f84-511e-9051-a0b1f3d7a6de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330032} {:answer/id #uuid "9e75d514-6353-5579-8d7e-665dc82b2915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330033} {:answer/id #uuid "519743e3-cd0c-557d-b755-5ccabca252cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330034} {:answer/id #uuid "23e70970-9b4c-56da-b14d-9fc32a8cbdd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "519743e3-cd0c-557d-b755-5ccabca252cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330034} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ddb7940-089d-513e-85e6-afc05b16a677" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9762f314-b655-58a9-a815-41bc2f93077e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49908" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}+x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+x=x(3x+1).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(3x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{(3x+1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2=A(3x+1)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A(3(-\\frac{1}{3})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ 2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}B \\\\ 6 & =-B \\\\ B & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A(3(0)+1)+B(0) \\\\ 2 & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(3x+1)}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{6}{3x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x(3x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{6}{3x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{3}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
